#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530} :activity/id #uuid "15829972-de8f-5398-aedc-ee127d53c955" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193827 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to use Riemann sums to approximate definite integrals of *composite* functions. As we'll see, this has some interesting applications.\n\nFor example, suppose $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous function. Some values for $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [1,5]$ are given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nLet's use this data to approximate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo approximate this definite integral, we first define a new function $g(x)$ as\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2\\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, our task is to approximate\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5-1}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(1) + g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nLet's add a row to our table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $g(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $9$ | $16$ | $50$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{1}^{5}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (3 + 8 + 9 + 16) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a right Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2502b79-d3d8-53e4-be62-b01752a16be9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6553 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1997ebaf-5bad-5fd7-875b-27650eb3f5b6" :step/math-academy-id 39566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192408 :question/id #uuid "d8b6c55c-c272-5818-a113-40e6218fe12c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "824c497f-a01d-53c5-bcfe-e6cd35e69a88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193067}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Riemann Sum of a Composite Function"} :step/id #uuid "99cb3798-47dd-5790-b2f7-fdfbcde6185f" :step/math-academy-id 39567 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192409 :question/id #uuid "c32c93a7-189d-5dc5-94a4-943ac5bbf29a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34555306-3c72-55ef-a59f-ab8d97ae4919" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503677}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "7001e0a2-bc0e-5f22-adfb-6688e2d5c0c7" :step/math-academy-id 39568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192410 :question/id #uuid "dbd945a6-786d-515e-8ecd-351dcf6b29f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9946876-7435-589a-a18d-c89c3d790754" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050438}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193028}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Riemann Sum Gives an Overestimate or an Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "d04db767-8034-5d9f-8871-0fb87a4da642" :step/math-academy-id 39569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192420 :question/id #uuid "54c6bc65-515f-52dd-8c37-ef6742464361" :question/math-academy-id "e-10520"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea7448d8-a235-52f4-a475-006943f8a142" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193068}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "b9769694-1018-5682-8dd1-facf2627a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 39706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193831 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use Riemann sums to approximate areas under curves of composite functions. Our final task is determining whether these approximations overestimate or underestimate the true areas.\n\nWe start by considering *right* Riemann sums. Recall the following facts:\n\n- If a function is strictly *increasing*, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If a function is strictly *decreasing*, the right Riemann sum *underestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x \\in [1,2]$, and differentiable for $x \\in (1,2)$. Now, consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSuppose we use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x)$ over $x\\in [1,2]$. Does the right Riemann sum overestimate or underestimate the true area?\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Differentiating our expression for $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n$g^{′}(x)|= (x^{2})^{′} \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x); = 2x \\cdot f(x) + x^{2} \\cdot f^{′}(x)$.\nWe now examine the sign of each factor in this expression to determine the sign of $g'(x)$.Since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,2)$, we have\n$2x \\gt 0, \\qquad f(x) \\gt 0, \\qquad x^2 \\gt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$g'(x) = \\underbrace{2x}_{\\gt 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2}_{> 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{2x \\cdot f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2 \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} \\gt 0$.\n\nTherefore, since $g'(x) \\gt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a left Riemann sum." :tutorial/id #uuid "4017a527-6912-5d2d-a92d-3424c1399d67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6582 :tutorial/title "Determining Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "6cc57411-9166-578d-ad5d-18fd5906fee2" :step/math-academy-id 39707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193904 :tutorial/content "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/59/59f9a4f76889bf4323c7bcf7dad2dd5269698d94f294458eafdd4c5da0fa0580.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc2858aac81addf1d5c19cd3836361d42de4ce844117ab4d42960e45a274026d.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the volume $V$ of this solid of revolution is given by the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169b0392-4eed-5202-921f-c379f4264c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7613 :tutorial/title "Volumes of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "5ed00cb2-4d94-5a81-8e1c-488592b9d60c" :step/math-academy-id 44209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193905 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nThe length of the curve between these two points is known as the **arc length** of the curve.\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can use Riemann sums to approximate arc lengths. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c4aa50-1ce8-52f8-aa97-b2bce6c85623" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7614 :tutorial/title "Arc Length"} :step/id #uuid "4da87fab-f669-5510-93d3-55fea59cbf65" :step/math-academy-id 44210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193532}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192870}]}
